e\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The spiral similarity centred at }A\\text{ sends }C\\to E,\\; D\\to F.} \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and its reflection\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the circle \\((PMN)\\) is the image of the circle \\((ACD)\\) under the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{PA}{PC}=\\frac{PA}{PD}\\;(=1\\text{ by }(1)). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(D\\) to \\(F\\) (by (5)), and consequently sends the whole circle \\((PMN)\\) to the circle \\((AEF)\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the point \\(H'\\) we obtain a point  \n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal H(H')\\in (AEF). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H\\) is a homothety centred at \\(A\\), the line \\(AH'\\) is mapped onto the line \\(AX\\); but \\(AH'\\) is symmetric to \\(AH\\) with respect to \\(MN\\), and the symmetry axis is perpendicular to \\(AP\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\)\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) (they are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nTherefore \\(B\\in (AEF)\\).  \nFrom (8) we already have \\(X\\in (AEF)\\).  \nSince \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points \\(B,E,F,H\\) lie on the same circle, namely \\((BEF)\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}.} \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is an external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nRecall that a line through a vertex of a triangle is an external symmedian iff it is symmetric to the corresponding median with respect to the internal angle bisector at that vertex.  \n\nFrom (5) the spiral similarity at \\(A\\) sends the chord \\(CD\\) of \\((ACD)\\) to the chord \\(EF\\) of \\((AEF)\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\), their images \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by (9)).  \nHence the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\).  \n\nThe median of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) from vertex \\(E\\) is the line joining \\(E\\) to the midpoint of \\(BF\\).  \nUnder the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\), the median from \\(C\\) of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) is carried to the median from \\(E\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nSince the line \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\) (a well\u2011known property of the configuration of two intersecting circles), its image \u2013 the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  \n\nFinally, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with this external symmedian (both pass through the point \\(X\\) defined in (8) and are parallel to \\(AP\\)).  \n\nA fundamental property of a triangle states that the external symmedian at a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the point where the symmedian meets the circle. Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)