ral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nB\\in (AEF). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) we already have \\(X\\in (AEF)\\).  Because \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points  \n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nlie on the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  We record\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}.} \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma: the line of centres is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\)\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line of their centres meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  Then the line \\(MN\\) joining the centres is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MB=MC\\); similarly \\(NB=ND\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MB}{MC}= \\frac{NB}{ND}=1 . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  By the intersecting‑chords theorem applied to the two circles we have  \n\n\\[\nBX\\cdot XD = (BM^{2}-r_{M}^{2}) = (BN^{2}-r_{N}^{2}) = CX\\cdot XM . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nDividing (14) by \\(CX^{2}\\) and using \\(MB=MC,\\; NB=ND\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XD}= \\frac{BC^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nCondition (15) is the well‑known metric characterisation of the external symmedian of a triangle: a cevian through a vertex is the external symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  Hence \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom the Lemma, \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C\\).  \nThe spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) established in Step 1 sends  \n\n\\[\nC\\longmapsto E,\\qquad D\\longmapsto F,\\qquad MN\\longmapsto \\text{the line through }A\\text{ parallel to }AP . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the image of the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  By (10) this image is precisely the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); because \\(HX\\) is also parallel to \\(AP\\) (Step 2), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{HX\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle BEF\\text{ at }E}. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Tangency\n\nA basic property of a triangle states that the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the point where the symmedian meets the circle.  Since \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the external symmedian \\(HX\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (Step 3), the line \\(HX\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(X\\).\n\nBecause \\(HX\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), we have proved the required statement:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n∎"
