            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, the radius of \\(\\Omega\\) being smaller;\n* \\(A,B\\) the two distinct intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\neq M\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\neq N\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));\n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe have to show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(CD\\) to \\(EF\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles with vertex \\(A\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP = \\angle PAD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle CAP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) and \\(F\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF = \\angle DAP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5) is precisely the condition that the unique spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) which sends the segment \\(CD\\) to the segment \\(EF\\) maps  \n\n\\[\nC\\longmapsto E,\\qquad D\\longmapsto F . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and its reflection\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the line \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{PA}{PC}= \\frac{PA}{PD}=1 \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nmaps the circle \\((PMN)\\) onto the circle \\((AEF)\\).  \nBy (6) the same homothety sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(D\\) to \\(F\\); consequently it sends the whole circle \\((PMN)\\) to \\((AEF)\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the point \\(H'\\) we obtain a point  \n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal H(H')\\in (AEF). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H\\) is a homothety centred at \\(A\\), the image of the line \\(AH'\\) is the line \\(AX\\); but \\(AH'\\) is the reflection of \\(AH\\) in the line \\(MN\\), and the axis of that reflection is perpendicular to \\(AP\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nB\\in (AEF). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) we already have \\(X\\in (AEF)\\).  Because \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points  \n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nlie on the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  We record\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}.} \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma: the line of centres is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\)\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line of their centres meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  Then the line \\(MN\\) joining the centres is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MB=MC\\); similarly \\(NB=ND\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MB}{MC}= \\frac{NB}{ND}=1 . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  By the intersecting‑chords theorem applied to the two circles we have  \n\n\\[\nBX\\cdot XD = (BM^{2}-r_{M}^{2}) = (BN^{2}-r_{N}^{2}) = CX\\cdot XM . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nDividing (14) by \\(CX^{2}\\) and using \\(MB=MC,\\; NB=ND\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XD}= \\frac{BC^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nCondition (15) is the well‑known metric characterisation of the external symmedian of a triangle: a cevian through a vertex is the external symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  Hence \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom the Lemma, \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C\\).  \nThe spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) established in Step 1 sends  \n\n\\[\nC\\longmapsto E,\\qquad D\\longmapsto F,\\qquad MN\\longmapsto \\text{the line through }A\\text{ parallel to }AP . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the image of the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  By (10) this image is precisely the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); because \\(HX\\) is also parallel to \\(AP\\) (Step 2), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{HX\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle BEF\\text{ at }E}. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Tangency\n\nA basic property of a triangle states that the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the point where the symmedian meets the circle.  Since \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the external symmedian \\(HX\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (Step 3), the line \\(HX\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(X\\).\n\nBecause \\(HX\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), we have proved the required statement:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
