\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete, rigorous solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n   Hence the unique spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends the segment \\(CD\\) to the segment \\(EF\\) maps \\(C\\mapsto E\\) and \\(D\\mapsto F\\).\n\n2. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the line \\(MN\\).  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle, so \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).\n\n3. **Homothety centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   Since \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{PA}{PC}=1\\) sends the circle \\((PMN)\\) onto the circle \\((AEF)\\) and sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\), \\(D\\) to \\(F\\).  \n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal H(H')=:X\\) lies on \\((AEF)\\) and the line \\(AX\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Thus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\).**  \n   Both circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the original circles, therefore \\(B\\in (AEF)\\).  Since \\(X\\in (AEF)\\) as shown above, the four points \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic; we denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).\n\n5. **The line \\(HX\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   A classical lemma states that for two intersecting circles the line joining their centres is the external symmedian of the triangle formed by the two intersection points and the two points where