\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles with vertex \\(A\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP = \\angle PAD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE = \\angle CAP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) and \\(F\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF = \\angle DAP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5) is precisely the condition that the unique spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) which sends the segment \\(CD\\) to the segment \\(EF\\) maps  \n\n\\[\nC\\longmapsto E,\\qquad D\\longmapsto F . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and its reflection\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the line \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{PA}{PC}= \\frac{PA}{PD}=1 \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nmaps the circle \\((PMN)\\) onto the circle \\((AEF)\\).  \nBy (6) the same homothety sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(D\\) to \\(F\\); consequently it sends the whole circle \\((PMN)\\) to \\((AEF)\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the point \\(H'\\) we obtain a point  \n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal H(H')\\in (AEF). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H\\) is a homothety centred at \\(A\\), the image of the line \\(AH'\\) is the line \\(AX\\); but \\(AH'\\) is the reflection of \\(AH\\) in the line \\(MN\\), and the axis of that reflection is perpendicular to \\(AP\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nB\\in (AEF). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) we already have \\(X\\in (AEF)\\).  Because \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points  \n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nlie on the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  We record\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}.} \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma: the line of centres is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\)\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line of their centres meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  Then the line \\(MN\\) joining the centres is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MB=MC\\); similarly \\(NB=ND\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MB}{MC}= \\frac{NB}{ND}=1 . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  By the intersecting‑chords theorem applied to the two circles we have  \n\n\\[\nBX\\cdot XD = (BM^{2}-r_{M}^{2}) = (BN^{2}-r_{N}^{2}) = CX\\cdot XM . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nDividing (14) by \\(CX^{2}\\) and using \\(MB=MC,\\; NB=ND\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XD}= \\frac{BC^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nCondition (15) is the well‑known metric characterisation of the external symmedian of a triangle: a cevian through a vertex is the external symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of