(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the line \\(AH'\\) to the line \\(AX\\) and, by (3), sends the line \\(AP\\) to itself, the image of the line symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to \\(MN\\) is a line symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to the image of \\(MN\\).  Because the image of \\(MN\\) is a line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (see (3)), we obtain\n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nB\\in (AEF). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we already have \\(X\\in (AEF)\\).  Because \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points\n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nlie on the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lemma – the line of centres is an external symmedian\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line joining their centres meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  Then the line of centres is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote the centres by \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and set \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MB=MC\\); similarly \\(NB=ND\\).  \n\nApplying the power‑of‑a‑point theorem to \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles gives\n\n\\[\nBX\\cdot XD = MX^{2}-r_{M}^{2}=NX^{2}-r_{N}^{2}=CX\\cdot XM . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nDividing (9) by \\(CX^{2}\\) and using \\(MB=MC,\\;NB=ND\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XD}= \\frac{BC^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEquation (10) is the defining metric property of the **external** symmedian of a triangle: a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  Hence \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Transport of the external symmedian to \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) of step 1 to the configuration \\((B,C,D,\\,MN)\\).  \nBy (3) we have \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\;\\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)=\\ell\\), where \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (see (6)).  \n\nA spiral similarity preserves cross‑ratios, therefore it sends the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (which is \\(MN\\) by the Lemma) to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency\n\nA standard property of a triangle states that its external symmedian through a vertex meets the circum‑circle at the point where the tangent at that vertex’s antipode touches the circle; equivalently, the external symmedian is tangent to the circum‑circle at its second intersection with the circle.\n\nSince the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((BEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\) (step 3) and we have just proved that this line is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), it follows that it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(X\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), completing the proof. ∎"
