 Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently \\(\\angle CAP=\\angle PAC\\) and \\(\\angle DAP=\\angle PAD\\).\n\nSince \\(C,E\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) meets that circle at \\(C\\) and \\(E\\), we have \\(\\angle CAE=\\angle CAP\\).  \nSimilarly \\(\\angle DAF=\\angle DAP\\).\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF), \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition that the unique spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) sending segment \\(CD\\) to segment \\(EF\\) also sends\n\n\\[\nC\\longmapsto E,\\qquad D\\longmapsto F,\\qquad AP\\longmapsto AP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDenote this similarity by \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The reflected orthocenter\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circum‑circle; thus\n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of \\(H'\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)\n\nThe circles \\((PMN)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) have the same centre on the line \\(AP\\) and the same radius:\n\n* The centre of \\((PMN)\\) is the midpoint of the segment joining the circum‑centres of \\(\\triangle PM\\) and \\(\\triangle PN\\); because \\(PA=PC=PD\\), this midpoint lies on \\(AP\\).\n* The centre of \\((AEF)\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(EF\\) of the circle through \\(A,E,F\\); again it lies on \\(AP\\).\n\nMoreover, by (1) the radii of the two circles are equal to \\(PA\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the whole circle \\((PMN)\\) onto \\((AEF)\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the point \\(H'\\) we obtain a point\n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\in (AEF). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the line \\(AH'\\) to the line \\(AX\\) and, by (3), sends the line \\(AP\\) to itself, the image of the line symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to \\(MN\\) is a line symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to the image of \\(MN\\).  Because the image of \\(MN\\) is a line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (see (3)), we obtain\n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nB\\in (AEF). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we already have \\(X\\in (AEF)\\).  Because \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points\n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nlie on the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lemma – the line of centres is an external symmedian\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line joining their centres meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  Then the line of centres is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote the centres by \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and set \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MB=MC\\); similarly \\(NB=ND\\).  \n\nApplying the power‑of‑a‑point theorem to \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles gives\n\n\\[\nBX\\cdot XD = MX^{2}-r_{M}^{2}=NX^{2}-r_{N}^{2}=CX\\cdot XM . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nDividing (9) by \\(CX^{2}\\) and using \\(MB=MC,\\;