spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(AP)=AP .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Image of the circle \\((PMN)\\))\n\nFrom (1) the circle \\((PMN)\\) has centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the ray \\(AP\\) and preserves distances from \\(P\\) (it is a composition of a rotation about \\(A\\) and a homothety with centre \\(A\\) of factor \\(\\dfrac{PA}{PA}=1\\)), it maps the whole circle \\((PMN)\\) onto the circle \\((AEF)\\), which also has centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((PMN)\\bigr)=(AEF). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3 (Reflection of the orthocentre)\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circum‑circle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to \\(H'\\).  By (3) the image  \n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\n\\]\n\nlies on \\((AEF)\\).  Moreover, because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the line \\(AH'\\) to the line \\(AX\\) and sends the line \\(MN\\) (which is the reflection of \\(AP\\) in the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)) to a line through \\(A\\) that is the reflection of \\(AP\\) in the same bisector, we obtain\n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 4 (Points \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic)\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two original intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); hence \\(B\\in (AEF)\\).  \nSince \\(X\\in (AEF)\\) and \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points  \n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nlie on the same circle, namely the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Denote this circle by \\((BEF)\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}}.\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 5 (External symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line of their centres meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  Then the line of centres \\(MN\\) is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(X=MN\\cap BD\\).  Because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres, \\(MB=MC\\) and \\(NB=ND\\).  Power of a point applied to \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles gives  \n\n\\[\nBX\\cdot XD = MX^{2}-R_{M}^{2}=NX^{2}-R_{N}^{2}=CX\\cdot XM .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDividing (7) by \\(CX^{2}\\) and using \\(MB=MC,\\;NB=ND\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XD}= \\frac{BC^{2}}{CD^{2}} .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) is precisely the metric characterisation of the **external** symmedian: a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  Hence \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 6 (Transport of the external symmedian)\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the configuration \\((B,C,D,\\,MN)\\) to \\((B,E,F,\\,\\ell)\\), where \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by (5)).  \nA spiral similarity preserves cross‑ratios, therefore it sends the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (which is \\(MN\\) by Lemma 5) to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 7 (External symmedian is tangent)\n\nIn any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex meets the circum‑circle at the point where the tangent drawn at the *antipode* of that vertex 