of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), and by \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n> Then the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**3. PROOF**\n\nWe work in the Euclidean plane and use directed angles modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.1  A spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP=\\angle PAD .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,E\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) meets that circle at \\(C\\) and \\(E\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle CAP .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(D,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF=\\angle DAP .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF),\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition for the existence of a unique **spiral similarity** \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending segment \\(CD\\) to segment \\(EF\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}\\text{ fixes the ray }AP .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.2  The circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); consequently the perpendicular bisectors of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) intersect on the line \\(AP\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nO\\in AP .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(OP=OA\\) because \\(O\\) is equidistant from \\(P\\) and from the two points \\(M,N\\) which are themselves symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\). Using (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\nOA=OP=PA .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the circle \\((PMN)\\) has centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(PA\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.3  Mapping of circles\n\nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the ray \\(AP\\) at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(A\\) (by (1) and the definition of \\(P\\)). Consequently\n\n\\[\nOE=OF=OA=PA .\n\\]\n\nHence the circle \\((AEF)\\) has the same centre \\(O\\) and the same radius \\(PA\\) as \\((PMN)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the centre \\(O\\) (it fixes the ray \\(AP\\) and sends a point at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(A\\) to another point at the same distance), we have\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((PMN)\\bigr)=(AEF). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.4  The reflected orthocentre\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle; therefore\n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to \\(H'\\). From (9) and (10) we obtain\n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\in (AEF). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nA spiral similarity sends a line through its centre to a line through the centre of the similarity. Since \\(MN\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\), its image is a line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell\\). Thus\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)=\\ell,\\qquad \\ell\\parallel AP,\\ \\ell\\text{ passes through }A. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AH'\\) is symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to \\(MN\\); applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) and using (12) we obtain that the image line \\(AX\\) is symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to a line parallel to \\(AP\\). The only line through \\(A\\) having this property is a line parallel to \\(AP\\) itself; hence\n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.5  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nB\\in (AEF). \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nFrom (11) we already have \\(X\\in (AEF)\\). Since \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points\n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nlie on the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.6  Lemma – the line of centres is an external symmedian  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line joining their centres meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  Then the line of centres is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote the centres by \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). Because \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MB=MC\\); similarly \\(NB=ND\\).  \n\nApply the power‑of‑a‑point theorem to the intersection \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\) with respect to the two circles:\n\n\\[\nBX\\cdot XD = MX^{2}-r_{M}^{2}=NX^{2}-r_{N}^{2}=CX\\cdot XM . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nWriting the directed lengths on line \\(BD\\) and using \\(MB=MC,\\ NB=ND\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XD}= \\frac{CB^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nEquation (17) is precisely the metric characterisation of the **external** symmedian of a triangle: a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  Hence \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C