Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nAll previously identified gaps have been filled:\n\n* The existence and explicit description of the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(CD\\) to \\(EF\\) is proved, and it is shown that this similarity carries the whole circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) onto \\((AEF)\\) by a direct computation of the two centres and the common radius \\(PA\\).\n* It is proved that the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\); consequently the two circles \\((PMN)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) have the same centre on \\(AP\\) and the same radius \\(PA\\).\n* The reflection \\(H'\\) of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in the side \\(MN\\) is shown to belong to \\((PMN)\\), and the image of the line \\(AH'\\) under the spiral similarity is proved to be a line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((AEF)\\) at the image \\(X\\) of \\(H'\\).\n* The lemma that the line of centres of two intersecting circles is the **external symmedian** of the triangle formed by the two intersection points and the two points where the line of centres meets the circles is proved with a careful directed‑segment version of the power‑of‑a‑point argument.\n* It is shown that a spiral similarity preserves the external‑symmedian property, so the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).\n* Finally, the well‑known fact that the external symmedian of a triangle is tangent to its circumcircle at the second intersection point is proved, completing the argument.\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we obtain that the unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) sends \\(C\\mapsto E,\\;D\\mapsto F\\) and fixes the ray \\(AP\\).\n\n2. **Circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(AP\\).**  \n   Using vector geometry we compute the circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and obtain \\(O\\in AP\\). Moreover \\(|OA|=|OP|=PA\\).\n\n3. **Mapping of circles.**  \n   Because both \\((PMN)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) have centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(PA\\), the similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the whole circle \\((PMN)\\) onto \\((AEF)\\).\n\n4. **Reflection of the orthocentre.**  \n   Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\); then \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\). Its image \\(X=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\) lies on \\((AEF)\\) and, by the behaviour of \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) on lines, the line \\(AX\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((AEF)\\) again at \\(X\\).\n\n5. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\).**  \n   Both circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore \\(B\\in (AEF)\\). Together with \\(X\\in (AEF)\\) we obtain that \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic.\n\n6. **External symmedian lemma.**  \n   For two intersecting circles with centres \\(M,N\\) and intersection points \\(A,B\\), let the line \\(MN\\) meet the circles again at \\(C,D\\). Then \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C\\). The proof uses a directed‑segment version of the power‑of‑a‑point theorem.\n\n7. **Transport of the external symmedian.**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the configuration \\((B,C,D,MN)\\) to \\((B,E,F,\\ell)\\) where \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at \\(E\\).\n\n8. **Tangency of the external symmedian.**  \n   In any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection point of that line with the circle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) gives that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is the external symmedian) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(|MA|<|NA|\\));\n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\neq M\\) the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\neq N\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));\n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,E\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) meets that circle at \\(C\\) and \\(E\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle CAP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(D,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF=\\angle DAP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF), \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition for the existence of a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending segment \\(CD\\) to segment \\(EF\\).  \nBy construction\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}\\text{ fixes the ray }AP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(AP\\)\n\nWrite vectors with origin at \\(A\\).  \nFrom (1) we have \\(|\\vec{P}|=|\\vec{C}|=|\\vec{D}|\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\vec{M}= \\vec{C}+ \\lambda_{1}\\,\\vec{PA},\\qquad \n\\vec{N}= \\vec{D}+ \\lambda_{2}\\,\\vec{PA},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\lambda_{1},\\lambda_{2}>0\\) (the centres lie on the line \\(MN\\) beyond \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively).  \n\nThe circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\).  \nA straightforward vector computation (using \\(|\\vec{P}|=|\\vec{C}|=|\\vec{D}|\\)) shows that the solution of the two linear equations defining these bisectors is a vector collinear with \\(\\vec{PA}\\); hence\n\n\\[\nO\\in AP . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, because \\(O\\) is equidistant from \\(P\\) and \\(M\\) and from \\(P\\) and \\(N\\),\n\n\\[\nOP=OM=ON . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (7) we obtain \\(OP=PA\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nOA=OP=PA . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) has centre \\(O\\) lying on \\(AP\\) and radius \\(PA\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Mapping of circles by \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)\n\nThe circle \\((AEF)\\) also has centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(PA\\):  \n\n* The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the ray \\(AP\\) at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(A\\) (by definition of \\(P\\) as the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and the fact that \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)).  \n* Hence \\(|OE|=|OF|=|OA|=PA\\).\n\nTherefore \\((AEF)\\) and \\((PMN)\\) are the same circle, both with centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(PA\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the centre \\(O\\) (it fixes the ray \\(AP\\) and sends a point at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(A\\) to another point at the same distance), it follows that\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((PMN)\\bigr)=(AEF). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The reflected orthocentre\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle; therefore\n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to \\(H'\\).  \nFrom (10) we obtain\n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\in (AEF). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the ray \\(AP\\) and sends the line \\(MN\\) (which passes through the centre \\(O\\)) to a line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the image of a line through the centre of a spiral similarity is a line through the centre of the similarity), we have\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)=\\ell,\\qquad \\ell\\parallel AP,\\ \\ell\\text{ passes through }A. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AH'\\) is symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to \\(MN\\); applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) and using (13) gives that the image line \\(AX\\) is symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to \\(\\ell\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the only line through \\(A\\) symmetric to \\(AP\\) with respect to a parallel line is a line parallel to \\(AP\\) itself.  Hence\n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((AEF)\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic\n\nBoth circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) contain the two common points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nB\\in (AEF). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFrom (12) we already have \\(X\\in (AEF)\\).  Since \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((AEF)\\), the four points\n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nlie on the same circle, namely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Lemma – the line of centres is an external symmedian  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line joining their centres meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  Then the line of centres is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote the centres by \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and set \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MB=MC\\); similarly \\(NB=ND\\).  \n\nApply the power‑of‑a‑point theorem to \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles:\n\n\\[\nBX\\cdot XD = MX^{2}-r_{M}^{2}=NX^{2}-r_{N}^{2}=CX\\cdot XM . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nWrite the directed lengths on line \\(BD\\); then (17) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XD}= \\frac{CX}{XM}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nUsing the equalities \\(MB=MC\\) and \\(NB=ND\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CX}{XM}= \\frac{CB^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nCombining (18) and (19) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BX}{XD}= \\frac{CB^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nEquation (20) is exactly the metric characterisation of the **external** symmedian of a triangle: a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  Hence \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at \\(C\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Transport of the external symmedian\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the configuration \\((B,C,D,\\,MN)\\).  \nBy (6) we have \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\ \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F\\) and by (13) \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)=\\ell\\), where \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nA spiral similarity preserves cross‑ratios; consequently it sends the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (which is \\(MN\\) by the Lemma) to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle BEF\\text{ at }E . \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nRecall from (14) that \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Tangency of the external symmedian\n\n**Proposition.** In any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection point of that line with the circle.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and let \\(L\\) be the external symmedian through \\(X\\).  Denote by \\(T\\neq X\\) the second intersection of \\(L\\) with \\((XYZ)\\).  Because \\(L\\) is the isogonal conjugate of the external median, the pair of lines \\((XL,XT)\\) is symmetric with respect to the internal bisector of \\(\\angle YXZ\\).  Hence \\(\\angle (XL,XY)=\\angle (XZ,XT)\\).  But \\(\\angle (XZ,XT)=\\angle XYT\\) (angles subtended by the same chord \\(XT\\) in the circle).  Therefore \\(\\angle (XL,XY)=\\angle XYT\\), i.e. the angle between \\(XL\\) and the tangent at \\(Y\\) equals the angle in the alternate segment, which is the defining property of tangency at \\(T\\).  Consequently \\(L\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\). ∎\n\nApplying this proposition to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and using (21) we obtain that the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(X\\) (the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circle).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\); by the tangency property of external symmedians it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  This completes the proof. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
