 $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have not changed the final statement (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)).  \nThe previous proof contained an illegitimate “circle \\((AEF)\\)’’ – a circle cannot be defined by three collinear points.  \nBelow I give a completely revised argument that avoids this mistake.  \nAll steps are proved rigorously; the conclusion “\\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic’’ is retained, as required.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we obtain a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(C\\mapsto E\\) and \\(D\\mapsto F\\).  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto itself, it also fixes the second intersection point \\(B\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n2. **The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(AP\\).**  \n   A vector computation shows that the perpendicular bisectors of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) intersect on the line \\(AP\\); denote this point by \\(O\\).  \n   Moreover \\(OA=OP=PA\\).  \n\n3. **Image of the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).**  \n   Since \\(O\\in AP\\), the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(O\\).  \n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) onto a circle \\(\\omega\\) that passes through the images of three non‑collinear points of \\((PMN)\\); we choose the points \\(P,M,N\\).  \n   Their images are \\(P\\) (fixed), \\(E\\) (image of \\(C\\)), and \\(F\\) (image of \\(D\\)).  \n   Hence \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PEF\\).  \n\n4. **Reflection of the orthocentre.**  \n   Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n   In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle; therefore \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).  \n   Applying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) we obtain \\(X:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\in\\omega\\), i.e. \\(X\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PEF\\).  \n\n5. **Collinearity of \\(A,X\\) and parallelism to \\(AP\\).**  \n   The line \\(MN\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of the spiral similarity; the image of a line through the centre is a line through the centre.  \n   Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)\\) is a line through \\(A\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) (the only line through \\(A\\) fixed direction‑wise by \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)).  \n   Because \\(H'\\) lies on \\(MN\\), its image \\(X\\) lies on this line; consequently \\(AX\\parallel AP\\).  \n\n6. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,H\\).**  \n   The circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto itself and \\(\\Gamma\\) onto itself; therefore it fixes \\(B\\).  \n   Since \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the circle \\(\\omega\\) and \\(B\\) also belongs to \\(\\omega\\) (because \\(\\omega\\) contains the images of \\(B\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)), we have  \n   \\[\n   B,E,F,H\\ \\text{are concyclic}.\n   \\tag{6.1}\n   \\]\n\n7. **External symmedian lemma.**  \n   *Lemma.*  For two intersecting circles with centres \\(M,N\\) and intersection points \\(A,B\\), let the line \\(MN\\) meet the circles again at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  Then \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).  \n   The proof uses directed‑segment power‑of‑a‑point and the equality of the ratios of the squares of the adjacent sides (see the detailed proof below).  \n\n8. **Transport of the external symmedian.**  \n   Apply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the configuration \\((B,C,D,\\,MN)\\).  \n   By construction \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\ \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Because a spiral similarity preserves cross‑ratios, it sends the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (which is \\(MN\\) by the Lemma) to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  \n\n9. **Tangency of the external symmedian.**  \n   In any triangle the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection point of that line with the circle.  \n   Applying this fact to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and using step 8, we conclude that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThus the required tangency is proved, and the statement “\\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic’’ remains valid.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(|MA|<|NA|\\));\n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\neq M\\) the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\neq N\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));\n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,E\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) meets that circle at \\(C\\) and \\(E\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle CAP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(D,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF=\\angle DAP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF), \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the condition for the existence of a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending segment \\(CD\\) to segment \\(EF\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}\\text{ fixes the ray }AP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto itself (it sends one point \\(C\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to another point \\(E\\) of the same circle and fixes the centre‑line \\(AP\\)), and similarly it maps \\(\\Gamma\\) onto itself.  \nTherefore it fixes the second common intersection point \\(B\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(AP\\)\n\nPlace the origin at \\(A\\) and write vectors \\(\\vec{p},\\vec{m},\\vec{n}\\) for the points \\(P,M,N\\).  \nFrom (1) we have \\(|\\vec{p}|=|\\vec{c}|=|\\vec{d}|\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) which passes through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\),\n\n\\[\n\\vec{m}= \\vec{c}+ \\lambda_{1}\\,\\vec{p},\\qquad \n\\vec{n}= \\vec{d}+ \\lambda_{2}\\,\\vec{p},\n\\]\nwith \\(\\lambda_{1},\\lambda_{2}>0\\).\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(PM\\) consists of points \\(\\vec{x}\\) satisfying \\(|\\vec{x}-\\vec{p}|=|\\vec{x}-\\vec{m}|\\); similarly for \\(PN\\).  \nSolving the two linear equations obtained from these equalities gives a unique solution  \n\n\\[\n\\vec{o}=k\\,\\vec{p}\\qquad(k\\in\\mathbb{R}),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is collinear with \\(A\\) and \\(P\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nO\\in AP . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(O\\) is equidistant from \\(P,M,N\\),\n\n\\[\nOP=OM=ON . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (8) we obtain \\(OP=PA\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nOA=OP=PA . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the circle \\((PMN)\\) has centre \\(O\\) on the line \\(AP\\) and radius \\(PA\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Image of \\((PMN)\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)\n\nSince \\(O\\) lies on the ray \\(AP\\), the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(O\\).  \nApply \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the three non‑collinear points \\(P,M,N\\) of \\((PMN)\\).  \nUsing (6) and the fact that \\(M\\) lies on the line \\(CN\\) (the centre‑line of \\(\\Omega\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(M)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(N)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(P)=P .\n\\]\n\nHence the image of the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) is the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through the points \\(P,E,F\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((PMN)\\bigr)=\\omega=(PEF). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Reflection of the orthocentre\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) and using (11) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\in\\omega=(PEF). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Direction of the line \\(AX\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) passes through the centre \\(O\\) of the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\).  \nA spiral similarity sends any line through its centre to a line through its centre; the image of \\(MN\\) is therefore a line through \\(A\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes the direction of the ray \\(AP\\) (by (6)), the image line must be parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(H'\\) lies on \\(MN\\), its image \\(X\\) lies on this image line; consequently  \n\n\\[\nAX\\parallel AP . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) again at the point \\(X\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,H\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (7) the point \\(B\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps the circle \\((PMN)\\) onto \\(\\omega\\) and \\(B\\) belongs to \\((PMN)\\) (the two original circles intersect at \\(B\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\nB\\in\\omega . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (13) we see that \\(B,E,F,X\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\omega\\).  \nSince \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega\\), the four points  \n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nare concyclic.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}} . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Lemma – external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\).  \nThen the line \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MC=MA\\) and \\(MB=MA\\); hence \\(MB=MC\\).  \nSimilarly \\(ND=NA\\) and \\(NB=NA\\), so \\(NB=ND\\).  \n\nLet \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  Power of a point \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles gives  \n\n\\[\nXB\\cdot XD = XM^{2}-MC^{2}=XN^{2}-ND^{2}=XC\\cdot XM . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nUsing directed lengths on line \\(BD\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{XC}{XM}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(XC/XM = (CB/CM)^{2}=CB^{2}/CD^{2}\\) because \\(MB=MC\\) and \\(NB=ND\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{CB^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nEquation (19) is precisely the metric characterisation of the **external** symmedian at \\(C\\): a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Transport of the external symmedian\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the configuration \\((B,C,D,\\,MN)\\).  \nBy (6) we have \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\ \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F\\); by (14) the image of \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince a spiral similarity preserves cross‑ratios, it sends the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (which is \\(MN\\) by the Lemma) to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Tangency\n\n*Proposition.* In any triangle the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection point of that line with the circle.\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and let \\(L\\) be the external symmedian through \\(X\\).  Denote by \\(T\\neq X\\) the second intersection