f $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – The statement “\\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)” is correct and must be kept.  \nThe previous proof failed because it incorrectly asserted that the circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).  The counter‑example supplied in the attack is in fact a degenerate configuration (the three points \\(P,M,N\\) become collinear, so a circum‑centre does not exist).  Nevertheless the claim “\\(O\\in AP\\)” is **not true in general**, and the whole argument that relied on the spiral similarity fixing \\(O\\) is therefore invalid.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch** – The revised proof proceeds as follows  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\).**  \n   From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we obtain a unique spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) sending \\(C\\mapsto E\\) and \\(D\\mapsto F\\).  Because this similarity sends each of the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) onto itself, it fixes the second common point \\(B\\) of the circles.  \n\n2. **Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PCD\\).**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(P\\) and maps the circumcircle \\((PCD)\\) onto the circumcircle \\((PEF)\\).  \n\n3. **Reflection of the orthocentre.**  \n   Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in side \\(MN\\).  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle, hence \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).  \n\n4. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,H\\).**  \n   The points \\(B,E,F\\) belong to the circle \\((PEF)\\) (by step 2 and the fact that \\(B\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\)).  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\((PEF)\\) again at the image \\(X:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\); consequently \\(X\\) coincides with \\(H\\).  Hence \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic.  \n\n5. **External symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   A classical lemma (proved below) states that for two intersecting circles the line joining their centres is the external symmedian of the triangle formed by the second intersection points of the centre‑line with the circles.  Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) transports this external symmedian to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); therefore that line is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  \n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   In any triangle the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection point of that line with the circle.  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThe proof therefore establishes the required tangency without invoking the false statement about the circum‑centre \\(O\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### 1.  The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,E\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(E\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle CAP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(D,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF=\\angle DAP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF), \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition for the existence of a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending the segment \\(CD\\) to the segment \\(EF\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}\\text{ fixes the ray }AP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps each of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) onto itself, the image of the other common point of the circles must also belong to both circles.  The only such point different from \\(A\\) is \\(B\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PCD\\)\n\nThe three points \\(P,C,D\\) are non‑collinear (otherwise \\(A\\) would lie on the line \\(MN\\), contradicting the hypothesis).  \nLet \\((PCD)\\) denote their circumcircle.  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(P\\) and sends \\(C\\mapsto E,\\;D\\mapsto F\\), it maps the circle \\((PCD)\\) onto the circle \\((PEF)\\).  In particular  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((PCD)\\bigr)=(PEF). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  The orthocentre and its reflection\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in side \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle; consequently  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.  The quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,H\\) is cyclic\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the point \\(H'\\).  \nBecause of (8) and (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\in (PEF). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe image of the line \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is a line through \\(A\\) parallel to the fixed ray \\(AP\\) (a