ion \\((B,C,D,\\,MN)\\).  \nBy (6) we have \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\ \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F\\); by (11) the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nA spiral similarity preserves cross‑ratios, hence it sends the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (which is \\(MN\\) by the Lemma) to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  \n\n#### 7.  Tangency of the external symmedian\n\n*Proposition.* In any triangle the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection point of that line with the circle.\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and let \\(L\\) be the external symmedian through \\(X\\).  Denote by \\(T\\neq X\\) the second intersection of \\(L\\) with \\((XYZ)\\).  The external symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external median; therefore the pair of lines \\((XL,XT)\\) is symmetric with respect to the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle YXZ\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (XL,XY)=\\angle (XZ,XT)=\\angle XYT ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality follows from the inscribed‑angle theorem.  Thus \\(\\angle (XL,XY)=\\angle XYT\\), which is precisely the condition for \\(L\\) to be tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\). ∎\n\nApplying this proposition to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and using the result of step 6, we conclude that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion** – The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\); by the external‑symmedian tangency property it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\tr