l lemma (proved below) states that for two intersecting circles the line joining their centres is the external symmedian of the triangle formed by the second intersection points of the centre‑line with the circles.  Applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) transports this external symmedian to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); therefore that line is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  \n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   In any triangle the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection point of that line with the circle.  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThe proof therefore establishes the required tangency without invoking the false statement about the circum‑centre \\(O\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### 1.  The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); in particular  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP=\\angle PAD . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,E\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(E\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle CAP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(D,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF=\\angle DAP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF), \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition for the existence of a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending the segment \\(CD\\) to the segment \\(EF\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}\\text{ fixes the ray }AP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps each of the circles \\(\\Omega