}} . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.  Lemma – external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet two circles with centres \\(M,N\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line \\(MN\\) meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\) (order \\(C-M-N-D\\)).  Then the line \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) at vertex \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MC=MA\\) and \\(MB=MA\\); hence \\(MB=MC\\).  Similarly \\(ND=NA\\) and \\(NB=NA\\), so \\(NB=ND\\).  \n\nLet \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  Power of a point \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles gives  \n\n\\[\nXB\\cdot XD = XM^{2}-MC^{2}=XN^{2}-ND^{2}=XC\\cdot XM . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities in (13) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{XC}{XM}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(MB=MC\\) and \\(NB=ND\\), the ratios \\(\\dfrac{XC}{XM}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{CB}{CD}\\) are equal; squaring both sides of (14) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{CB^{2}}{CD^{2}} . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nEquation (15) is the metric characterisation of the **external** symmedian at \\(C\\): a cevian through a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides.  ∎\n\n\n\n#### 6.  Transport of the external symmedian\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the configuration \\((B,C,D,\\,MN)\\).  \nBy (6) we have \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\ \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F\\); by (11) the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nA spiral similarity preserves cross‑ratios, hence it sends the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (which is \\(MN\\) by the Lemma) to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at vertex \\(E\\).  \n\n#### 7.  Tangency of the external symmedian\n\n*Proposition.* In any tr