.\n\nThus the required tangency is proved, and the auxiliary concyclicity \\(B,E,F,H\\) follows as a by‑product. ∎\n\n\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP=\\angle PAC ,\\qquad \\angle DAP=\\angle PAD .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,E\\) lie on the same circle \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) at \\(C\\) and again at \\(E\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle CAP .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, because \\(D,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle DAF=\\angle DAP .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AC,AE)=\\angle (AD,AF),\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the condition for the existence of a unique spiral similarity\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}:\\;A\\longmapsto A\n\\]\nsending the segment \\(CD\\) to the segment \\(EF\\).  Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S_{A}\\text{ fixes the ray }AP .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) maps each of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) onto itself, the image of the other common point of the circles must also belong to both circles.  The only such point different from \\(A\\) is \\(B\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(B)=B .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  Image of the circumcircle \\((PCD)\\)\n\nThe three points \\(P,C,D\\) are non‑collinear (otherwise \\(A\\) would lie on the line \\(MN\\), contradicting the hypothesis).  \nLet \\((PCD)\\) denote their circumcircle.  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) fixes \\(P\\) and sends \\(C\\mapsto E,\\;D\\mapsto F\\), it maps the circle \\((PCD)\\) onto the circle \\((PEF)\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}\\bigl((PCD)\\bigr)=(PEF). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  The orthocentre and its reflection\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocentre across a side lies on the circumcircle; consequently  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.  Concatenation: \\(B,E,F,H\\) are concyclic  \n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the point \\(H'\\).  \nFrom (9) and (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nX:=\\mathcal S_{A}(H')\\in (PEF). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe image of the line \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to the fixed ray \\(AP\\).  \nIndeed, let \\(M_{1}=\\mathcal S_{A}(M)\\) and \\(N_{1}=\\mathcal S_{A}(N)\\).  Because \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the circle centred at \\(M\\) to itself, the segment \\(AM_{1}\\) is obtained from \\(AM\\) by a rotation of angle \\(\\angle BAE\\); the same rotation sends \\(AN\\) to \\(AN_{1}\\).  Hence \\(\\angle M_{1}AN_{1}= \\angle MAN\\).  Since \\(M,N\\) lie on the line through \\(A\\) making angle \\(\\angle MAN\\) with the ray \\(AP\\), the line \\(M_{1}N_{1}\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)\\;=\\; \\ell\\quad\\text{with}\\quad \\ell\\parallel AP\\text{ and }A\\in\\ell .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(H'\\) lies on \\(MN\\), its image \\(X\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\); i.e. \\(AX\\parallel AP\\).  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\((PEF)\\) at the point \\(X\\) defined above, so this line indeed passes through \\(X\\).  \n\nFrom (7) we have \\(B\\in(PEF)\\) as well.  Hence the four points  \n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nare concyclic.  This proves  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}} .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.  External‑symmedian lemma  \n\n*Lemma.*  Let two circles with centres \\(M,N\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Let the line \\(MN\\) meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second circle again at \\(D\\) (order \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\)).  Then the line \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) issued from \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Set \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  By the power‑of‑a‑point theorem applied to \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles we have  \n\n\\[\nXB\\cdot XD = XM^{2}-MC^{2}=XN^{2}-ND^{2}=XC\\cdot XM .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MC=MA\\) and \\(MB=MA\\); thus \\(MB=MC\\).  Likewise \\(NB=ND\\).  Dividing the two equalities in (13) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{XC}{XM}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(MB=MC\\) and \\(NB=ND\\), the triangles \\(XMB\\) and \\(XMC\\) are isosceles, whence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XC}{XM}= \\frac{CB}{CD}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nCombining (14) and (15) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{CB}{CD}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{CB^{2}}{CD^{2}}, \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the metric definition of the **external symmedian** through \\(C\\) in \\(\\triangle BCD\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n#### 6.  Transport of the external symmedian  \n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) to the configuration of the lemma.  \nBy (6) we have \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(C)=E\\) and \\(\\mathcal S_{A}(D)=F\\); by (11) the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince a spiral similarity preserves cross‑ratios, it carries the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (the line \\(MN\\)) to the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;\\text{ is the external symmedian of }\\triangle BEF\\text{ issued from }E .\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(\\ell\\) passes through the orthocentre \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(\\ell\\).\n\n#### 7.  Tangency of the external symmedian  \n\n*Proposition.*  In any triangle the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection point of that line with the circle.\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\) and let the external symmedian through \\(X\\) meet the circumcircle again at \\(T\\neq X\\).  By definition of an external symmedian,\n\\[\n\\frac{TB}{TC}= \\frac{XB^{2}}{XC^{2}} .\\tag{19}\n\\]\nApplying the law of sines in \\(\\triangle XBT\\) and \\(\\triangle XCT\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{TB}{TC}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle XTB}{\\sin\\angle XTC}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle XTB}{\\sin\\angle XTC}.\n\\]\nBut \\(\\angle XTB\\) and \\(\\angle XTC\\) are supplementary (they subtend the same arc \\(XT\\) of the circumcircle), hence \\(\\sin\\angle XTB=\\sin\\angle XTC\\).  Equation (19) therefore forces \\(\\angle XTB=\\angle XTC\\), i.e. \\(\\angle (XT,XT)=\\angle XTB\\).  This is exactly the condition that the line \\(XT\\) be tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).  ∎\n\nApplying the proposition to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and using (18) we conclude that the line \\(\\ell\\) – which is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the point \\(E\\).\n\nHence the required tangency is proved, and together with (12) we have established all statements of the problem. ∎"
