}(N)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the circle centred at \\(M\\) onto itself, the segment \\(AM_{1}\\) is obtained from \\(AM\\) by the same rotation that sends \\(C\\) to \\(E\\); similarly \\(AN_{1}\\) is the image of \\(AN\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle M_{1}AN_{1}= \\angle MAN .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) makes with the ray \\(AP\\) the angle \\(\\angle MAN\\) (the centres \\(M,N\\) lie on the line through the two intersection points \\(A,B\\) of the circles).  \nSince the rotation that defines \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the ray \\(AP\\) onto itself, (9) shows that the line \\(M_{1}N_{1}\\) is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) and passes through \\(A\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{A}(MN)=\\ell,\\qquad \\ell\\parallel AP,\\;A\\in\\ell .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(H'\\) lies on \\(MN\\), its image \\(X\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\); i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\((PEF)\\) at the point \\(X\\).  \n\nFinally, (4) gives \\(B\\in (PEF)\\).  Hence the four points  \n\n\\[\nB,\\;E,\\;F,\\;H\n\\]\n\nare concyclic.  This proves  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}} .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.  External‑symmedian lemma  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet two circles with centres \\(M,N\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nLet the line \\(MN\\) meet the first circle again at \\(C\\) and the second again at \\(D\\) (order \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\)).  \nThen the line \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) issued from \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet \\(X:=MN\\cap BD\\).  \nPower of a point \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles gives  \n\n\\[\nXB\\cdot XD = XM^{2}-MC^{2}= XN^{2}-ND^{2}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MC=MA\\) and \\(MB=MA\\); hence \\(MB=MC\\).  \nSimilarly \\(NB=ND\\).  Using these equalities in (12) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{XM}{XN}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nNow consider triangles \\(XMB\\) and \\(XMC\\).  They share the side \\(