es on the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the image of \\(H'\\) after a reflection across \\(MN\\) and the similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) is exactly the point where that line meets \\((PEF)\\)).  \n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\((PEF)\\) at a point of the circle, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   B,E,F,H\\text{ are concyclic}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **External symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\).**  \n   **Lemma.** In the configuration of two intersecting circles with centres \\(M,N\\) and common points \\(A,B\\), let \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with the first (resp. second) circle. Then the line \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BCD\\) issued from \\(C\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\(X=MN\\cap BD\\).  Power of a point \\(X\\) with respect to the two circles gives  \n\n   \\[\n   XB\\cdot XD = XM^{2}-MC^{2}= XN^{2}-ND^{2}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(M\\) is the centre of the first circle, \\(MC=MA=MB\\); similarly \\(ND=NB\\).  Substituting in (9) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{XM}{XN}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   In triangles \\(XMB\\) and \\(XMC\\) we have \\(MB=MC\\) and a common side \\(XM\\); thus they are congruent, giving  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{XM}{XB}= \\frac{XC}{XD}= \\frac{CB}{CD}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (10) and (11) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{XB}{XD}= \\frac{CB^{2}}{CD^{2}} .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (12) is precisely the metric characterisation of the **external symmedian** through \\(C\\) in \\(\\triangle BCD\\) (the external symmedian divides the opposite side in the ratio of the squares of the adjacent sides). ∎\n\n7. **Transport of the external symmedian.**  \n   The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends \\(C\\mapsto E\\), \\(D\\mapsto F\\) and, by (6), sends the line \\(MN\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Since a spiral similarity pres