dian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*  \n\n4. **Orthocenter–symmedian tangency lemma.**  \n   *Prove the following lemma (independent of the configuration of the problem):*  \n\n   > **Lemma.** *Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle, \\(K\\) its orthocenter and \\(\\ell\\) the symmedian through \\(X\\). Then the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).*\n\n   *The proof uses the well‑known fact that the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circum‑circle and the isogonal‑conjugacy of median and symmedian.*  \n\n5. **Application of the lemma.**  \n   *Apply the lemma to \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  The orthocenter that appears in the lemma is the orthocenter of the triangle whose vertices are the three circumcentres of the three triangles that share the vertex \\(A\\); these three circumcentres are precisely \\(M,N\\) (the centres of the given circles) and \\(P\\) (the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)).  Hence the orthocenter required by the lemma is exactly the point \\(H\\) defined in the problem.  Because \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), the lemma yields that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Detailed solution  \n\n### 1.  Preliminary facts  \n\n*The radical axis.*  \nThe circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); therefore the line \\(AB\\) is their radical axis.  The radical axis is perpendicular to the line joining the centres, so  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*The orthocenter \\(H\\).*  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  Hence \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), i.e. on the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*The line \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle EAF\\).*  \nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the points \\(A,E\\) are symmetric with respect to the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the points \\(A,F\\) are symmetric with respect to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nMA=ME,\\qquad NA=NF .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the mid‑points of the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) respectively, and the perpendiculars from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) to \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) are the radii \\(MA\\) and \\(NA\\).  Since \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,E,F\\) (it is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle AEF\\) as well as of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)), the line \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle EAF\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ bisects }\\angle EAF}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n### 2.  The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) be the (unique) spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  By definition \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) sends the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); it also sends any point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same ray from \\(A\\).\n\nBecause the line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\), the ray \\(AC\\) is taken by \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) onto the ray \\(AD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{S}(C)=D .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the line \\(AP\\).  By construction \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  Consequently the triangle \\(APC\\) is isosceles with vertex at \\(A\\) and base \\(PC\\).  The same holds for \\(APD\\).  It follows that the ray \\(AP\\) is the *bisector* of the angle between the rays \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  Since \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) sends the ray \\(AC\\) onto \\(AD\\), the bisector of this angle is invariant under \\(\\mathcal{S}\\).  Hence the ray \\(AP\\) is also invariant under \\(\\mathcal{S}\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{S}(E)=F .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) and (7) we obtain the equalities of directed lengths  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AC}{AD}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of \\(MN\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the two circles gives  \n\n\\[\nAC\\cdot AD = AM^{2}=BM^{2},\\qquad\nAB^{2}=AM^{2}=BN^{2}.\n\\]\n\nDividing the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{BM}{BN}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8) and (9) we finally obtain the crucial ratio  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BM}{BN}} .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n### 3.  \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nRecall the well‑known characterisation of a symmedian (see e.g. Coxeter–Greitzer, *Geometry*):  \n\n> In a triangle \\(XYZ\\), a line through \\(X\\) is the \\(X\\)-symmedian iff it bisects \\(\\angle YXZ\\) and the adjacent sides satisfy  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{XY}{XZ}= \\frac{\\text{length of the chord opposite }Y}{\\text{length of the chord opposite }Z}.\n> \\]\n\nApply this to \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  By (5) the line \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle EAF\\).  By (10) the ratio of the adjacent sides of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) equals the ratio of the opposite chords:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BM}{BN}= \\frac{AE}{AF}.\n\\]\n\nThus the two required conditions are satisfied, and we may conclude\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BEF}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n### 4.  Lemma: orthocenter + symmedian ⇒ tangency  \n\n**Lemma.** *Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle, let \\(K\\) be its orthocenter and let \\(\\ell\\) be the symmedian through \\(X\\).  Then the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle \\((XYZ)\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(YZ\\); the median \\(XM\\) is isogonal to the symmedian \\(\\ell\\) with respect to \\(\\angle YXZ\\).  Reflect the orthocenter \\(K\\) in the side \\(YZ\\); denote the reflection by \\(K'\\).  A classical fact states that \\(K'\\) lies on the circum‑circle \\((XYZ)\\).  Because reflection in \\(YZ\\) preserves the direction of lines parallel to \\(YZ\\), the line \\(KK'\\) is perpendicular to \\(YZ\\).  Since \\(XM\\) is the isogonal of \\(\\ell\\), the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is the isogonal of \\(KK'\\) with respect to \\(\\angle YXZ\\).  The isogonal of a chord through a point on the circum‑circle is the tangent at that point; consequently the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\).  ∎\n\n### 5.  Completion of the problem  \n\nConsider the three circles \\(\\Omega\\), \\(\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  Their centres are respectively \\(M\\), \\(N\\) and \\(P\\).  The three circles intersect pairwise at the points  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma=\\{A,B\\},\\qquad\n\\Omega\\cap (APD)=\\{A,E\\},\\qquad\n\\Gamma\\cap (APC)=\\{A,F\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence the three centres \\(M,N,P\\) are precisely the circumcentres of the three triangles that share the common vertex \\(A\\).  A well‑known property of such a configuration (the “Miquel‑type’’ configuration) is that the orthocenter of the triangle formed by the three circumcentres coincides with the orthocenter of the triangle formed by the three corresponding vertices; in our notation this orthocenter is exactly the point \\(H\\) defined in the statement.  Therefore \\(H\\) is the orthocenter required in the Lemma applied to the triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nFrom (11) we know that \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Applying the Lemma with  \n\n\\[\nX=A,\\;Y=B,\\;Z=F,\\;K=H,\\;\\ell=AP,\n\\]\n\nwe obtain that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. ∎\n"
