ma’’) tells us that \\(K'\\) lies on \\((XYZ)\\).  Moreover \\(KK'\\perp YZ\\).  \n\n   Because \\(XM\\) is isogonal to \\(\\ell\\), the isogonal of the line \\(KK'\\) (which is perpendicular to \\(YZ\\)) is precisely the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\).  In a triangle, the isogonal of a chord through a point on the circumcircle is the tangent at that point.  Since \\(K'\\) belongs to the circumcircle, the isogonal of \\(KK'\\) is the tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K'\\).  Consequently the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is that tangent.  ∎\n\n7. **Application of Lemma 6.1.**  \n   The three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) intersect pairwise at the points\n   \\[\n   \\Omega\\cap\\Gamma=\\{A,B\\},\\qquad\n   \\Omega\\cap (APD)=\\{A,E\\},\\qquad\n   \\Gamma\\cap (APC)=\\{A,F\\}.\n   \\]\n   Hence the centres \\(M,N,P\\) are the circum‑centres of the three triangles that share the common vertex \\(A\\).  A standard Miquel‑type result (the “orthocentre of the triangle of centres’’) asserts that the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle MNP\\) coincides with the orthocentre of the triangle formed by the three opposite vertices, i.e. with the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  By definition this orthocentre is the point \\(H\\) of the problem.  \n\n   From (4) we know that \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Applying Lemma 6.1 to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with\n   \\[\n   X=A,\\;Y=B,\\;Z=F,\\;K=H,\\;\\ell=AP,\n   \\]\n   we obtain that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved for every admissible configuration. ∎\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*All steps are exactly those outlined in the sketch above, written out in full detail and with every claim justified.*\n\n1. **Radical axis.**  \n   Since the circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the line \\(AB