   \\[\n   AC\\cdot AD = AM^{2}=BM^{2},\\qquad\n   AB^{2}=AM^{2}=BN^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Dividing the first equality by the second gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AC}{AD}= \\frac{BM}{BN}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Using (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{BM}{BN}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Chord‑ratio in \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the mid‑points of the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) of the two circles, the power of \\(B\\) with respect to the two circles yields\n   \\[\n   BE\\cdot BA = BM^{2},\\qquad BF\\cdot BA = BN^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Dividing gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BM}{BN}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Symmedian.**  \n   From (3) and (4) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}.\n   \\]\n   Together with the fact that \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle EAF\\) (proved in step 3), the characterisation of a symmedian yields that \\(AP\\) is the \\(A\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n8. **Orthocentre of \\(\\triangle MNP\\) equals orthocentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   The three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and \\((ACD)\\) intersect pairwise at the points \\(\\{A,B\\},\\{A,E\\},\\{A,F\\}\\).  By the Miquel theorem the circum‑centres of the three triangles \\(\\triangle ACD,\\triangle AEF,\\triangle ABM\\) are concyclic; the centre of that circle is precisely the nine‑point centre of \\(\\triangle MNP\\).  A well‑known consequence (see e.g. Coxeter–Greitzer, *Geometry*, Chap. 7) is that the orthocentre of the triangle formed by the three centres \\((M,N,P)\\) coincides with the orthocentre of the triangle formed by the three opposite vertices \\((B,E,F)\\).  Hence the point \\(H\\) defined in the statement is exactly the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n9. **Lemma (orthocentre + symmedian ⇒ tangency).**  \n   *Proof.*  Let \\(K\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(\\ell\\) be the symmedian through \\(X\\).  Let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(YZ\\); then \\(XM\\) is the median, which is the isogonal conjugate of \\(\\ell\\).  Reflect \\(K\\) across \\(YZ\\) to obtain \\(K'\\); the reflection lemma gives \\(K'\\in (XYZ)\\) and \\(KK'\\perp YZ\\).  Because isogonal conjugation preserves angles, the isogonal of the line \\(KK'\\) with respect to \\(\\angle YXZ\\) is the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\).  In a circle, the isogonal of a chord through a point on the circle is the tangent at that point; therefore the isogonal of \\(KK'\\) is the tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K'\\).  Consequently the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is that tangent.  ∎  \n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    Applying the lemma to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with orthocentre \\(H\\) (step 8) and symmedian \\(AP\\) (step 7) shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  This is exactly the statement to be proved. ∎"
